.TH smi-remote-exec "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-remote-exec \- Execute a command on a remote host over SSH
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-remote-exec
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENT " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-remote-exec
runs
.I COMMAND
with its
.IR ARGUMENT s
on a remote host using
.BR ssh (1).
It is used like
.BR sudo (8):
every argument belongs to the command, and the command runs as given, only on
the remote host.
.B smi-remote-exec
has no options of its own; the connection (host, port, user and password) is
taken only from environment variables, the same ones
.BR smi-upload (1)
reads.
.PP
Each argument reaches the remote command as exactly one argument.
Spaces, quotes,
.BR $ ,
.BR * ,
.B ;
and similar characters are passed on literally and are not interpreted by the
remote shell.
To use shell syntax such as pipes,
.BR && ,
redirections or variables on the remote host, run a shell explicitly:
.BR "smi-remote-exec sh -c 'cd /var/opt/a && ls'" .
.PP
An argument that is exactly
.B ~
or starts with
.B ~/
expands to the remote user's home directory.
Quote it locally,
.BR '~/deployments' ,
otherwise the local shell expands it to the local home directory before
.B smi-remote-exec
sees it.
A relative path is also resolved against the remote user's home directory.
.PP
Standard input is passed to the remote command, standard output and standard
error come back unchanged, and
.B smi-remote-exec
adds no output of its own, so it can be used in pipelines.
In a loop that reads its own standard input, redirect it,
.BR "smi-remote-exec COMMAND < /dev/null" ,
otherwise the remote command consumes the loop's input.
.PP
When a password is set, it is handed to
.BR sshpass (1)
through the
.SM SSHPASS
environment variable, never as a command line argument, so it does not appear
in the process list.
When no password is set,
.B ssh
runs on its own and key based authentication is used.
.PP
An unknown host key is accepted and stored without asking
.RB ( StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new ),
so the command works unattended.
A host key that has changed since it was stored is still refused.
.PP
No terminal is allocated on the remote host, so interactive programs and a
remote
.B sudo
that asks for a password do not work; use a
.B NOPASSWD
sudoers rule for commands run with
.BR "smi-remote-exec sudo" .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I COMMAND
Command to run on the remote host.
.TP
.I ARGUMENT
Arguments of the command, passed on one to one.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SMI_REMOTE_HOST
Remote host name or IP address.
Required, no default.
.TP
.B SMI_REMOTE_PORT
Remote SSH port.
Default:
.BR 22 .
.TP
.B SMI_REMOTE_USER
Remote user name.
Default: current OS login name.
.TP
.B SMI_REMOTE_PASSWORD
Remote user's password.
Default: empty, which means key based authentication without
.BR sshpass (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 1
Wrong usage, no host given, or a password is set but
.B sshpass
is not installed.
.TP
.I other
Exit status of the remote command.
.B ssh
returns
.B 255
when the connection itself fails;
.B sshpass
returns
.B 5
for a wrong password and
.B 6
for an unknown host key.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Move an uploaded archive into place:
.RS
.nf
export SMI_REMOTE_HOST=files.example.com SMI_REMOTE_USER=deploy
smi-upload build/a.tar.gz deployments/a.tar.gz
smi-remote-exec mv '~/deployments/a.tar.gz' /var/opt/a/deploy/a.tar.gz
.fi
.RE
.PP
Everything for one call, with a password and a non standard port:
.RS
.nf
SMI_REMOTE_HOST=files.example.com SMI_REMOTE_PORT=2222 \\
SMI_REMOTE_USER=deploy SMI_REMOTE_PASSWORD=secret \\
smi-remote-exec ls -l /var/opt/a/deploy
.fi
.RE
.PP
A file name with spaces stays one argument:
.RS
.nf
smi-remote-exec rm "/var/opt/a/deploy/old release.tar.gz"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Remote shell syntax through an explicit shell:
.RS
.nf
smi-remote-exec sh -c 'cd /var/opt/a/deploy && tar -xzf a.tar.gz'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Feed standard input to a remote command:
.RS
.nf
smi-remote-exec tee /var/opt/a/etc/app.config < app.config > /dev/null
.fi
.RE
